{
  "analysis": "The review describes an energy system that limits continued progression. Once the energy is depleted, the player cannot progress until an in-game recovery timer restores it, and the reviewer says that recovery takes a long time. This is an imposed wait timer that makes play follow the game's recharge schedule. The requests for a console version, a storyline, bigger maps, and more customization are hypothetical additions and are not coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "once you run out you can't really progress any further, and the timer is fairly long to recover fully",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "Depleted energy blocks further progress until an in-game timer restores it, forcing the player to wait according to the game's schedule."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes Poké Balls being consumed more quickly because Pokémon repeatedly break out during captures, leaving the player needing to buy more Balls. This is a resource-consumption pattern tied to continued gameplay, and the reviewer explicitly says purchasing more is necessary, which supports Pay to Progress. The review does not establish rigged capture outcomes; repeated breakouts and agreement from other players are not enough to show hidden manipulation. It also does not describe leftover currency, gambling, a purchase prompt, or a currency conversion. The final edit disputes authorship of the earlier text, but it does not identify a separate in-game mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "they keep breaking out so I go through the balls faster so I have to purchase more",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / definition",
      "rationale": "The consumable capture items are depleted and the reviewer states that purchasing more is required to continue the capture activity."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes progress being blocked by hard levels that may take a week to clear, but does not identify an in-game timer, regeneration schedule, or required repeated activity, so this does not support Playing by Appointment or Grinding. They also state that some tasks are impossible unless boosters are purchased, which makes payment the route to overcoming progress obstacles. The uninstall statement is an evaluation and does not add a label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "there are way too many impossible tasks unless you buy boosters",
      "rule_applied": "Free alternative",
      "rationale": "The span makes purchasing boosters necessary to complete otherwise impossible tasks, so payment is required to progress."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a time-limited event with a reward of 525 dice. They were three steps from earning it, ran out of dice, and saw that two days remained, but after returning later the event had changed and the reward opportunity disappeared. This is a game-imposed event schedule that determines when the player must complete progress, with the reward lost when the event changes. The mention of the F2P model is only a general concern because the reviewer says they never purchased anything; it does not explicitly describe payment being required for progress. The wasted time is also an evaluation, not by itself a separate mechanic.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "The latest event I was about to get 525 dice as reward, I had 3 left to go and I ran out of dice, it said 2Days left. Now just logged in after sleeping and the event is now changed.",
      "rule_applied": "vs Fear of Missing Out: Where a reward expires on the game's clock and the span describes the schedule or the loss, code Playing by Appointment.",
      "rationale": "The event operates on a fixed game schedule, and returning after the event changed caused the reviewer to lose access to the nearly completed reward."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a referral mechanic that is supposed to reward inviting friends and getting them to join Facebook, so the game uses social recruitment as an incentive. It also describes spending dice through a maximum-bet action for uncertain outcomes such as Shut downs and bank heists; the player reports using a large stake and receiving none, which supports a chance-based wagering mechanic. The repeated duplicate stickers describe a randomized reward pool producing unwanted duplicate items, which is Reward Mania. The inability to log in is explicitly attributed to an internet connection error, so it is a technical fault and does not support a dark-pattern label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "no rewards for inviting friends and getting them to join facebook",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme",
      "rationale": "The game ties rewards to recruiting friends and having them join through Facebook."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "when I finally had more than 500 dices and played max bet, absolutely NO LUCK! No Shut downs and no bank heists.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player describes staking a large amount of in-game dice on a maximum-bet chance mechanic with possible outcomes including Shut downs and bank heists."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "Stickers are duplicates all the way",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards",
      "rationale": "The sticker system delivers repeated unwanted duplicates from a randomized reward pool."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a deliberate interface behavior: opening the game or the 1v1 section automatically scrolls to and selects the highest-stakes table available, rather than the lowest or a neutral option. This steers the player toward spending more in-game coins and creates accidental mis-clicks that can make the player lose their coin balance. The reviewer then says that, after those losses, players are forced to buy more coins with real money, making payment an alternative required to continue playing. The interface manipulation itself is not an accidental real-money purchase, so M_EasyToPurchase is not supported. No chance-based outcome is described, so Gambling does not apply.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "it automatically scrolls and selects the highest possible bet table based on your coins",
      "rule_applied": "definition; attention-direction toward a costly option",
      "rationale": "The interface automatically steers the player toward the highest-cost table, directing selection away from the safer lower-bet option and facilitating accidental mis-clicks."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "players lose all their hard-earned coins and are forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall / definition",
      "rationale": "After the interface-induced coin loss, the review states that players must buy more coins with real money to keep playing."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_IntermediateCurrency",
      "span": "forced to buy real ones",
      "rule_applied": "Premium Currency; currency is the object acquired",
      "rationale": "The phrase describes purchasing additional in-game coins with real money, which is a real-money-to-virtual-currency conversion."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a chance-based game outcome system that they believe is deliberately unfavorable: the RNG is said to be “obviously against you,” which is an allegation that the game manipulates or predetermines outcomes rather than merely producing an unlucky streak. They also describe an in-game cost of 2200 at around level 100 and rewards that barely repay that cost, but this is an economy/value complaint rather than an explicit payment gate or real-money conversion. Saying the game is not rewarding without spending actual money does not by itself establish that payment is required for progress. No label is assigned for the low rewards because Reward Mania concerns how rewards are delivered, not simply their size or value.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "the rng is so obviously against you it's crazy",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging",
      "rationale": "The reviewer alleges that the game’s random outcomes are systematically manipulated against the player, describing concealed outcome determination rather than an ordinary losing streak."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a base-building game as repetitive, but “Same old same old grinder” is only a general characterization without a specific repeated activity tied to progress, so it does not meet the Grinding boundary. The reviewer explicitly describes events as competitions that ordinary players cannot win, and says payment is required or provides the winning advantage. The stated 30k spent to win the last event reinforces a monetized competitive structure, but does not by itself establish gambling, collection pressure, or another label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Zero skill pay to play. Zero skill pay to win.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win / Pay Wall",
      "rationale": "The span explicitly describes payment as necessary to participate or as the route to winning, making progress or competitive success contingent on spending."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "there is absolutely no opportunity for a general player to win an event",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes players competing in events for a win, which directly satisfies the competition definition."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes several active game mechanics. Spending money is presented as a route to competitive advantage, which supports Pay to Progress through the Pay to Win indicator. Base upgrades use increasingly long timers, imposing waits on advancement and supporting Playing by Appointment. The statement that everything scales endlessly and offers no meaningful endpoint describes an endlessly receding progression structure, supporting Infinite Treadmill, but 'more grind' does not name a repetitive activity clearly enough for Grinding. The UI is explicitly said to be confusing by design in order to push purchases, which supports UI Misdirection because the interface obstructs informed opt-out and serves a purchase-directed purpose. Alliances and player attacks establish player-versus-player competition, but the review does not state that attackers receive a reward for bullying outsiders, so Encourages Anti-Social Behavior is not assigned; nor is Forced Fellowship assigned because the alliance pressure is coercive grouping rather than an obligation to friends or teammates.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "it's set up in a pay to win way",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win",
      "rationale": "The review describes spending money as a way to gain an in-game competitive advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the update times for your base are becoming ridiculous",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play",
      "rationale": "Base upgrades are governed by in-game timers that delay further advancement."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Everything scales endlessly, there is kinda no point to look forward to",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "Progression is described as endlessly expanding without a meaningful attainable endpoint."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "The UI is deliberately confusing and overwhelming to achieve the effect that you need to buy stuff",
      "rule_applied": "vs UI complaint",
      "rationale": "The confusing interface is explicitly described as deliberate and purchase-directed, rather than merely being difficult to use."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "If you are not in one then you get attacked and bullied",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span describes players being put against one another through attacks by other players."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes a daily-reward system whose completion is conditional on wagering 50,000, so the daily reward is not simply granted for returning. They also describe timed rewards providing at most 40,000, creating a specific shortfall against the 50,000 requirement. Because the reviewer says they would need to pay to obtain the daily reward, payment is presented as the route around that in-game progress barrier. The betting mechanic is an explicit chance-based wager, but no real-money stake or purchased currency is described, so Gambling is not assigned. I did not search because the mechanics are clear from the review.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I can't get my daily rewards unless I bet 50,000 with out it I can't complete my daily rewards",
      "rule_applied": "Daily task lists with resetting rewards",
      "rationale": "The game presents a daily reward that can only be completed by meeting a wagering condition, making the daily reward a recurring daily-return mechanic."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "The most I can seem to get on the timed rewards is 40,000. I'd not pay just to get my daily rewards.",
      "rule_applied": "vs Free alternative",
      "rationale": "The free timed rewards fall 10,000 short of the 50,000 needed, and the reviewer identifies paying as the alternative for completing the daily reward."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_WasteAversion",
      "span": "The most I can seem to get on the timed rewards is 40,000",
      "rule_applied": "specific shortfall below an item's cost",
      "rationale": "The reviewer identifies a concrete 40,000 reward amount that is insufficient for the 50,000 wagering requirement, establishing a realized shortfall."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a sticker-collection goal that is effectively gated by expensive payment: the reviewer says the final stickers cannot be obtained without paying a lot, and that winning anything may cost hundreds of dollars. This supports Pay to Progress because payment is presented as necessary to advance or complete goals, and Complete the Collection because obtaining the last stickers is the stated objective. The review does not clearly describe a chance-based mechanic, a specific artificial scarcity rule, or a reward-delivery system, so Gambling, Deceptive Luxury, and Reward Mania are not assigned. The complaint about being required to give five stars to write a review concerns the store-review process rather than an in-game mechanic and is out of scope.",
